WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.016
Welcome to part two, where the component

2
00:00:03.056 --> 00:00:06.047
models of part one
become complete engines.

3
00:00:06.097 --> 00:00:10.070
We start with the separate-exhaust
turbofan and its two-spool

4
00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:14.083
power balance, then solve
exercise 1 A, the C F M fifty-six

5
00:00:14.123 --> 00:00:18.684
at cruise, and 1 B, the same
engine at take-off, step by step.

6
00:00:18.734 --> 00:00:23.418
Then we review propeller momentum theory
and the turboprop, and finish with the two

7
00:00:23.458 --> 00:00:28.476
conversions of the exercises: a propfan at
cruise and an open rotor on the ground.

8
00:00:30.126 --> 00:00:34.838
A separate-exhaust turbofan splits
the inlet flow into a core stream and

9
00:00:34.878 --> 00:00:40.112
a bypass, or secondary, stream, and
the bypass ratio alpha is their ratio.

10
00:00:40.162 --> 00:00:44.022
So the core flow is m dot zero
over one plus alpha, and the

11
00:00:44.062 --> 00:00:48.245
bypass flow is alpha times m
dot zero over one plus alpha.

12
00:00:48.295 --> 00:00:52.995
Fuel is referred to the core, so the
fuel flow is f times the core flow, and

13
00:00:53.035 --> 00:00:57.798
the T S F C carries a factor one plus
alpha in the denominator: forgetting it

14
00:00:57.838 --> 00:01:03.053
is, according to the manual, the most
frequent error in turbofan calculations.

15
00:01:03.103 --> 00:01:07.037
The turbofan has three design
variables where the turbojet had two:

16
00:01:07.077 --> 00:01:10.537
besides the compressor pressure
ratio and T t four, the fan

17
00:01:10.577 --> 00:01:15.376
pressure ratio and the bypass ratio,
which are coupled through the turbine.

18
00:01:15.426 --> 00:01:21.006
In exercise 1 A, with alpha equal to 6
and a total flow of 170 kilograms per

19
00:01:21.046 --> 00:01:28.814
second, the core receives 24.29 and the
bypass 145.71 kilograms per second.

20
00:01:30.464 --> 00:01:32.893
Real turbofans have two spools turning at

21
00:01:32.933 --> 00:01:36.596
different speeds, and the
exercises are posed that way.

22
00:01:36.646 --> 00:01:39.993
The high pressure spool is
simple: the high pressure turbine

23
00:01:40.033 --> 00:01:43.663
drives only the high pressure
compressor, so the compressor power

24
00:01:43.703 --> 00:01:48.118
equals eta m H times the turbine power,
which gives T t four point five.

25
00:01:48.168 --> 00:01:52.575
The low pressure turbine drives both the
fan and the low pressure compressor,

26
00:01:52.615 --> 00:01:57.644
working on gas already cooled by the high
pressure turbine, which gives T t five.

27
00:01:57.694 --> 00:02:01.440
Careful with the mass flows: the
fan compresses the whole inlet

28
00:02:01.480 --> 00:02:05.346
flow, while the low pressure
compressor only compresses the core;

29
00:02:05.386 --> 00:02:10.304
mixing the two conventions changes the
fan power by a factor one plus alpha.

30
00:02:10.354 --> 00:02:13.330
The overall pressure ratio
is the product of the three

31
00:02:13.370 --> 00:02:16.570
compression ratios, so in
exercise 1 A the high pressure

32
00:02:16.610 --> 00:02:21.709
compressor must provide 30.6
divided by 1.32 times 5, which is

33
00:02:21.749 --> 00:02:27.852
4.636, and each compressor starts from the
temperature the previous one left behind.

34
00:02:29.502 --> 00:02:32.355
Thrust now comes from
two streams, each with

35
00:02:32.395 --> 00:02:35.703
its own momentum, ram
drag and pressure term.

36
00:02:35.753 --> 00:02:39.897
The thrust ratio F R compares how
hard each stream works per unit of

37
00:02:39.937 --> 00:02:43.711
its own mass flow: it is the
ratio of specific thrusts, not of

38
00:02:43.751 --> 00:02:48.654
thrusts, and values around one minimise
fuel consumption in civil engines.

39
00:02:48.704 --> 00:02:51.690
The kinetic energy increment
adds both streams, and

40
00:02:51.730 --> 00:02:55.462
the thermal and propulsive
efficiencies follow as before.

41
00:02:55.512 --> 00:02:58.683
In the manual's worked
example, using the closed-form

42
00:02:58.723 --> 00:03:02.014
propulsive efficiency with
under-expanded nozzles loses

43
00:03:02.054 --> 00:03:06.462
eight percentage points, so always
compute it from the full thrust.

44
00:03:06.512 --> 00:03:10.661
In exercise 1 A, F R comes out at
about 10: the core stream works

45
00:03:10.701 --> 00:03:14.788
ten times harder per kilogram than
the fan stream, which says there

46
00:03:14.828 --> 00:03:19.552
is room for a larger bypass ratio,
exactly the trend of modern engines.

47
00:03:21.202 --> 00:03:25.093
Here is exercise 1 A, the
template for the whole partial.

48
00:03:25.143 --> 00:03:28.254
The C F M fifty-six has two
shafts: the high pressure

49
00:03:28.294 --> 00:03:31.583
turbine drives the high pressure
compressor, and the low

50
00:03:31.623 --> 00:03:34.853
